{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The cupbearer of the king of Egypt and the baker offended.</b> At first Scripture calls them 'the cupbearer' and 'the baker,' and afterward it says, ״וַיִּקְצֹף פַּרְעֹה עַל שְׁנֵי סָרִיסָיו עַל שַׂר הַמַּשְׁקִים וְעַל שַׂר הָאוֹפִים״ (Genesis 40:2). And some say that it is the way of the world that every officer has attendants beneath him who serve the king at all times, while they themselves serve him on occasion at a great banquet, as a mark of honor. And this offense was not committed by the officers themselves but by their attendants — the cupbearer and the baker — yet in any event the principal anger fell upon the officers for having seated ordinary men such as these, who were not careful with the king's honor. And what is said in connection with the dream — 'the cupbearer and the baker' — without mentioning the term 'officer,' is because Scripture did not come to indicate anything but the substance of the dream, each one according to his matter: this one according to the matter of cupbearing and that one according to the matter of baking." "And it appears to me to settle this along the lines of what we find in Bereshit Rabbah (88:2), where the Sages and Rabbi Evyatar disagree. The Sages say: a fly was found in his [the cupbearer's] cup, and a pebble was found in his [the baker's] loaf. And Rabbi Evyatar says: they sought to make advances upon the king's daughter, etc. And Rashi agreed with the words of the Sages. And it appears that his proof is from the fact that Scripture says 'cupbearer' and 'baker' and does not mention the term 'officer,' to indicate that their sin was not tied to their high office. For this sin — that they sought to make advances upon his daughter — is certainly tied to the fact that they were officers and dignitaries; therefore they sought to lay hands upon the king and to make advances upon his daughter because their hearts were lifted up unto corruption. Therefore Scripture calls them simply 'cupbearer' and 'baker' — both at the time of mentioning the sin and at the mention of the dream — with one reason for both: to indicate that their sin concerned matters of cupbearing and baking, and was not a sin tied to their high office. And so too in the dream it calls them thus, to indicate that the dream was about matters of cupbearing and baking." "And for this reason it says ״חָטְאוּ מַשְׁקֵה מֶלֶךְ מִצְרַיִם וְהָאוֹפֶה״ (Genesis 40:1), and it is difficult: why combine them and place them together — 'the cupbearer and the baker' — why did he insert 'the king of Egypt' between the joined words? Rather, surely it is to indicate that each one sinned in his own matter: this one with a fly and that one with a pebble. And as it appears, the negligence of the pebble is greater than the negligence of the fly, for a fly is force majeure while a pebble is negligence. Therefore it says 'the cupbearer of the king of Egypt': for had he done this to another man he would not be called negligent, for the matter is close to force majeure — but for a king it is negligence, for with the king's honor one must be meticulous to the utmost degree. But the sin of the baker was so great that it would have been so even had he done this to another man who is not a king; therefore it says simply 'and the baker,' and does not say 'the baker of the king of Egypt.' And ״פַּרְעֹה קָצַף עַל שַׂר הַמַּשְׁקִים״ (Genesis 40:2) — it mentions the term of office in connection with the anger: for just as the Holy One, blessed be He, is exacting with the righteous to a hair's breadth more than He is with any other man, so too these officers — by virtue of being officers and closest to the king — should have been careful with the king's honor. And had they been ordinary men, the anger would not have come upon them to such a degree. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:49.361791849Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}